### What is scaling and why does it exist?

Scaling exists because load grows: more users, more requests per second, more data. A system that cannot scale eventually saturates some resource and latency climbs until requests fail.

```java
package com.bank.scaling;

public class WhyScaling {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("Load growth at National Bank of India:");
        System.out.println("  Month 1:  1,000 users  ->  100 req/sec");
        System.out.println("  Month 6:  50,000 users ->  5,000 req/sec");
        System.out.println("  Month 12: 500,000 users -> 50,000 req/sec");
        System.out.println();
        System.out.println("Without scaling: CPU saturates, latency spikes, requests fail");
        System.out.println("With scaling: capacity grows with demand");
    }
}
```

```text
Expected output:
Load growth at National Bank of India:
  Month 1:  1,000 users  ->  100 req/sec
  Month 6:  50,000 users ->  5,000 req/sec
  Month 12: 500,000 users -> 50,000 req/sec

Without scaling: CPU saturates, latency spikes, requests fail
With scaling: capacity grows with demand
```

### How does vertical scaling work?

Vertical scaling means increasing the resources of a single node. You move the service from a small instance to a larger one. The application does not change at all.

```java
package com.bank.scaling;

public class VerticalScaling {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("Vertical scaling progression:");
        System.out.println("  t2.medium  (2 CPU, 4 GB)  -> handles 500 req/sec");
        System.out.println("  m5.xlarge  (4 CPU, 16 GB) -> handles 2000 req/sec");
        System.out.println("  m5.4xlarge (16 CPU, 64 GB)-> handles 8000 req/sec");
        System.out.println("  m5.24xlarge(96 CPU,384 GB)-> handles 40000 req/sec");
        System.out.println("  ??? -> no bigger machine exists. CEILING HIT.");
        System.out.println();
        System.out.println("Drawbacks:");
        System.out.println("  1. Hard ceiling: biggest machine money can buy is finite");
        System.out.println("  2. Downtime: resizing usually requires a restart");
        System.out.println("  3. Single point of failure: one machine dies, everything dies");
    }
}
```

```text
Expected output:
Vertical scaling progression:
  t2.medium  (2 CPU, 4 GB)  -> handles 500 req/sec
  m5.xlarge  (4 CPU, 16 GB) -> handles 2000 req/sec
  m5.4xlarge (16 CPU, 64 GB)-> handles 8000 req/sec
  m5.24xlarge(96 CPU,384 GB)-> handles 40000 req/sec
  ??? -> no bigger machine exists. CEILING HIT.

Drawbacks:
  1. Hard ceiling: biggest machine money can buy is finite
  2. Downtime: resizing usually requires a restart
  3. Single point of failure: one machine dies, everything dies
```

### How does horizontal scaling work?

Horizontal scaling means adding more instances and spreading load with a load balancer. The capacity ceiling is effectively removed because you keep adding nodes.

```java
package com.bank.scaling;

public class HorizontalScaling {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        int instanceCapacity = 2000; // req/sec per instance
        for (int instances = 1; instances <= 10; instances++) {
            int totalCapacity = instances * instanceCapacity;
            System.out.printf("  %2d instances -> %,d req/sec capacity%n",
                    instances, totalCapacity);
        }
        System.out.println();
        System.out.println("Benefits:");
        System.out.println("  1. Near-unlimited: keep adding nodes");
        System.out.println("  2. High availability: one instance dies, others serve");
        System.out.println("  3. No downtime: add/remove instances live");
        System.out.println();
        System.out.println("Prerequisite: service must be STATELESS");
        System.out.println("  State goes into Redis or the database");
    }
}
```

```text
Expected output:
   1 instances -> 2,000 req/sec capacity
   2 instances -> 4,000 req/sec capacity
   3 instances -> 6,000 req/sec capacity
   ...
  10 instances -> 20,000 req/sec capacity

Benefits:
  1. Near-unlimited: keep adding nodes
  2. High availability: one instance dies, others serve
  3. No downtime: add/remove instances live

Prerequisite: service must be STATELESS
  State goes into Redis or the database
```

### How do vertical and horizontal scaling compare?

| Aspect | Vertical Scaling | Horizontal Scaling |
|---|---|---|
| Approach | Bigger machine | More machines |
| Capacity ceiling | Limited by largest available hardware | Near-unlimited |
| Downtime to scale | Usually yes (restart) | No (add instances live) |
| Availability | Single point of failure | Redundancy built in |
| Application changes | None needed | Must be stateless |
| Cost curve | Exponential (bigger machines cost more) | Linear (add identical small machines) |
| Best for | Databases, in-memory caches | Stateless application tiers |

```java
package com.bank.scaling;

public class ScalingComparison {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("Vertical: simple, bounded, SPOF");
        System.out.println("Horizontal: complex, unlimited, HA");
        System.out.println();
        System.out.println("Real systems use BOTH:");
        System.out.println("  1. Size each instance sensibly (vertical)");
        System.out.println("  2. Scale out for elasticity and resilience (horizontal)");
    }
}
```

```text
Expected output:
Vertical: simple, bounded, SPOF
Horizontal: complex, unlimited, HA

Real systems use BOTH:
  1. Size each instance sensibly (vertical)
  2. Scale out for elasticity and resilience (horizontal)
```

### Why does horizontal scaling require statelessness?

If each instance holds its own session in local memory, requests cannot be freely distributed. State must go into a shared store like Redis or the database.

```java
package com.bank.scaling;

public class StatelessRequirement {
    // WRONG: Session stored in local memory
    // Map<String, UserSession> sessions = new HashMap<>();
    // Instance-1 stores Rajesh's session
    // Load balancer sends next request to Instance-2
    // Instance-2 has NO session for Rajesh -> login fails

    // CORRECT: Session stored in Redis
    // All instances read/write to shared Redis
    // Any instance can serve any request

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("WRONG: session in local memory");
        System.out.println("  Instance-1 has Rajesh's session");
        System.out.println("  Next request goes to Instance-2 -> session lost");
        System.out.println();
        System.out.println("CORRECT: session in Redis (or JWT)");
        System.out.println("  Any instance serves any request");
        System.out.println("  Scaling is free: just add instances");
    }
}
```

```text
Expected output:
WRONG: session in local memory
  Instance-1 has Rajesh's session
  Next request goes to Instance-2 -> session lost

CORRECT: session in Redis (or JWT)
  Any instance serves any request
  Scaling is free: just add instances
```

### When do you use vertical scaling?

Vertical scaling is the right choice for workloads that are inherently single-node, like a primary database or an in-memory cache that benefits from a larger heap.

```java
package com.bank.scaling;

import java.math.BigDecimal;

public class VerticalUseCases {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("Good vertical scaling use cases:");
        System.out.println("  1. Database primary: needs big disk, fast I/O");
        System.out.println("  2. In-memory portfolio calculator: needs large heap");
        System.out.println("  3. Single-node cache: more memory = more cache hits");
        System.out.println();
        System.out.println("When vertical hits the ceiling:");
        System.out.println("  Database -> shard (horizontal for data)");
        System.out.println("  Cache -> Redis Cluster (horizontal for cache)");
    }
}
```

```text
Expected output:
Good vertical scaling use cases:
  1. Database primary: needs big disk, fast I/O
  2. In-memory portfolio calculator: needs large heap
  3. Single-node cache: more memory = more cache hits

When vertical hits the ceiling:
  Database -> shard (horizontal for data)
  Cache -> Redis Cluster (horizontal for cache)
```

### How does Kubernetes automate horizontal scaling?

Kubernetes automates horizontal scaling with the Horizontal Pod Autoscaler, which watches metrics and changes the replica count to match demand.

```yaml
apiVersion: autoscaling/v2
kind: HorizontalPodAutoscaler
metadata:
  name: transfer-service-hpa
spec:
  scaleTargetRef:
    apiVersion: apps/v1
    kind: Deployment
    name: transfer-service
  minReplicas: 2
  maxReplicas: 20
  metrics:
    - type: Resource
      resource:
        name: cpu
        target:
          type: Utilization
          averageUtilization: 70
```

```text
HPA behavior:
  Normal load: 2 replicas at 40% CPU
  Month-end salary processing: CPU spikes to 85%
  HPA scales to 6 replicas, CPU drops to 50%
  Load subsides: HPA scales back to 2
  No human intervention, no downtime.
```

### What should a senior engineer know?

Horizontal scaling is not free lunch: it introduces coordination costs, data consistency challenges, and the need for distributed observability. Prefer horizontal scaling for the application tier and vertical scaling for inherently single-node workloads.

```text
Senior checklist:
  [x] Application tier: horizontal (stateless, behind LB)
  [x] Database: vertical first, then shard if needed
  [x] Cache: vertical first, then Redis Cluster
  [x] Instances sized sensibly (vertical baseline)
  [x] HPA for elastic scaling
  [x] Distributed observability for multi-instance debugging
  [x] Statelessness verified (no local sessions)
```
